\tag{fdip}
\title{The measure under a chiral rotation}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{The [paired degree scaling](fdip-00UD) multiplies the barred and
unbarred generators of each cell by a unit or its inverse, according to
cell parity. Its effect on the coefficient functional is computed
in \ref{fdip-00UF}. For a Ginsparg–Wilson operator, using the same
grading on both kinds of generators need not fix the bilinear.
Which rotation preserves it, and what factor does that rotation
give the coefficient functional?}

\p{Two gradings on a finite-dimensional vector space over a field
of characteristic different from #{2} suffice to define the rotation.}
\transclude{fdip-014J}
